# ETBZ reviewed secret-scanner findings.
#
# Each line is ONE finding fingerprint — `commit:path:rule:line` — that a human
# has inspected and accepted. A fingerprint is the narrowest exemption gitleaks
# offers: it pins the commit, the file, the rule AND the line, so an entry here
# cannot exempt a different value, a later edit of the same line, or the same
# string appearing anywhere else. No rule is disabled and no path is excluded.
#
# A line may be added under exactly one of three bars, and nothing else.
#
#   A — SYNTHETIC FIXTURE, ALREADY REWRITTEN. The flagged value exists only to
#       prove a guard works, and has since been rewritten so the CURRENT working
#       tree no longer contains it. Such an entry exempts history only, and the
#       working-tree gate stays green without it.
#
#   B — RECOVERED ARTEFACT, PINNED BY HASH. The flagged value is a CONTENT HASH
#       inside a vendored artefact that the repository preserves byte for byte,
#       and the file's SHA-256 is asserted in
#       `tests/contract/etbz49-visual-assets.contract.test.ts` via
#       `assets/visual-system-v1/ASSET-INTEGRITY.json`. This is what makes the
#       exemption safe in the working tree: the fingerprint pins a line, and the
#       hash pin means that line cannot change to anything else without a test
#       turning red first. An entry under bar B without that hash pin is not
#       permitted.
#
#   C — PUBLIC CONTENT DIGEST IN PINNED EVIDENCE (Product Owner decision,
#       2026-10-02, ETBZ-58). The flagged value is a public content digest (the
#       SHA-256 of a producer's OpenAPI document) inside a committed evidence
#       file, and a contract test pins BOTH the file's SHA-256 as a literal AND
#       that the only credential-shaped key/value pairs in the file are that
#       digest. The file pin is what makes a whole-line fingerprint safe for a
#       one-line JSON file: the file cannot change - not even by re-generating it
#       consistently - without editing the test. Written without a commit, like
#       bar B. An entry under bar C without both pins is not permitted.
#
# `scripts/secret-scan.sh` scans the working tree and the history as two
# separate gates. A bar-A entry carries a commit and covers history only. A
# bar-B entry is written WITHOUT a commit (`path:rule:line`): gitleaks matches
# that global fingerprint in the working-tree (`--no-git`) gate and in the
# history gate alike, so one line covers both — measured under gitleaks 8.30.1
# locally and the CI-pinned 8.24.3 (entry removed: both gates red; restored:
# both green).
